---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Building a Production-Ready CI/CD Pipeline"
  description="Design a CI/CD pipeline that goes from commit to production safely: build, test, scan, deploy, and roll back, with the guardrails that keep a bad change from reaching users."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-content">
      <a class="back" href="/guides/">← Back to Guides</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">DevOps · Guide</span>
      <h1>Building a Production-Ready CI/CD Pipeline</h1>
      <p>
        A pipeline that just runs is easy to build. A pipeline that
        catches problems before your users do, and lets you recover
        quickly when one slips through, takes deliberate design.
      </p>
      <div class="meta"><span>13 min read</span><span>·</span><span>Tool-agnostic — applies to Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and others</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#stages">The five stages every pipeline needs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#build">Build: produce one immutable artifact</a></li>
        <li><a href="#test">Test: fail fast, in the right order</a></li>
        <li><a href="#scan">Scan: catch known vulnerabilities before deploy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#deploy">Deploy: progressive rollout, not a big bang</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rollback">Rollback: decide the criteria before you need them</a></li>
        <li><a href="#secrets">Handling secrets without leaking them</a></li>
        <li><a href="#speed">Keeping the pipeline fast enough that people trust it</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="stages">The five stages every pipeline needs</h2>
    <p>
      Regardless of which CI/CD platform you use, a production-grade
      pipeline separates into five stages that each answer a different
      question: build (does the code compile into something?), test (does
      it work?), scan (is it safe to run?), deploy (is it live?), and
      verify (did the deployment actually succeed?). Skipping straight
      from build to deploy is how untested, unscanned code reaches
      production.
    </p>

    <h2 id="build">Build: produce one immutable artifact</h2>
    <p>
      The single most important rule of the build stage: build the
      artifact once, then promote that exact same artifact through every
      later environment. Never rebuild from source at each stage — a
      "rebuild" between staging and production, even from the same commit,
      can produce a subtly different artifact if a dependency was
      re-resolved or a base image moved.
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker build -t myapp:$GIT_SHA .
docker push registry.example.com/myapp:$GIT_SHA</code></pre>
    <p>
      Tag by commit SHA, not by a mutable label like <code>latest</code> or
      even a version number that could be reused. Every later stage
      references that exact tag, so "what's actually running in
      production" is always answerable with a single command instead of a
      guess.
    </p>

    <h2 id="test">Test: fail fast, in the right order</h2>
    <p>
      Order your test stages from fastest and cheapest to slowest and most
      expensive, and stop on the first failure. There's no reason to spend
      twenty minutes on integration tests if a linter would have caught
      the problem in five seconds.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Static analysis and linting</strong> — seconds, catches syntax and style issues.</li>
      <li><strong>Unit tests</strong> — fast, isolated, no external dependencies.</li>
      <li><strong>Integration tests</strong> — slower, exercises real dependencies like a database.</li>
      <li><strong>End-to-end tests</strong> — slowest, but proves the whole system works together.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Run independent test suites in parallel where your CI platform
      supports it — sharding a large test suite across several runners is
      usually the single biggest speed win available without touching a
      line of test code.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scan">Scan: catch known vulnerabilities before deploy</h2>
    <p>
      A dependency or container scan belongs in the pipeline, not as a
      separate quarterly audit — by the time a quarterly scan finds a
      critical CVE, it may have already been running in production for
      months. Set the gate to block on high-confidence, high-severity
      findings only; a scanner that blocks every build on low-severity
      noise gets disabled by frustrated engineers within a month, which is
      worse than not having it at all.
    </p>
    <pre><code># container image scan
trivy image registry.example.com/myapp:$GIT_SHA

# dependency scan
npm audit --audit-level=high</code></pre>

    <h2 id="deploy">Deploy: progressive rollout, not a big bang</h2>
    <p>
      Deploying to 100% of traffic at once means 100% of your users find
      out about a bad release at the same time. A progressive rollout —
      canary, blue-green, or a simple rolling update with health checks —
      contains the blast radius of anything that gets past your tests and
      scans.
    </p>
    <p>
      Whatever strategy you choose, the deploy stage must be able to
      answer one question automatically: is the new version actually
      healthy? A deployment that reports "success" because the process
      started, without checking that it's actually serving correct
      responses, is not a deployment you can trust.
    </p>
    <pre><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/myapp --timeout=120s
curl -f https://myapp.example.com/healthz</code></pre>

    <h2 id="rollback">Rollback: decide the criteria before you need them</h2>
    <p>
      Define what "this deployment failed" means before you ship, not
      while you're staring at a dashboard during an incident. A concrete
      threshold — error rate above X%, p99 latency above Y ms, health
      check failing for more than Z seconds — turns a stressful judgment
      call into a checklist.
    </p>
    <pre><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp</code></pre>
    <p>
      Practice the rollback path in a non-incident, so the first time your
      team runs it isn't also the first time they've ever run it. A
      rollback procedure that's only ever been tested in theory has a way
      of not working exactly when you need it most.
    </p>

    <h2 id="secrets">Handling secrets without leaking them</h2>
    <p>
      Secrets belong in a dedicated secrets manager, injected into the
      pipeline at runtime — never committed to the repository, never
      baked into the built image, and never printed to build logs. If a
      secret ever does reach a log or a commit, rotating it is not
      optional: treat it as compromised the moment it's exposed, because
      build logs and git history are effectively permanent and often
      readable by more people than you'd expect.
    </p>
    <pre><code># reference a secret at runtime, don't hardcode it
export DB_PASSWORD=$(vault kv get -field=password secret/myapp/db)</code></pre>

    <h2 id="speed">Keeping the pipeline fast enough that people trust it</h2>
    <p>
      A slow pipeline gets worked around — engineers start batching
      changes, skipping tests locally, or merging without waiting for CI,
      all of which quietly erode the safety the pipeline was supposed to
      provide. Watch pipeline duration as a metric over time, not just
      whether it passes, and treat a steadily growing build time as a
      problem to fix, not a fact of life.
    </p>
    <p>
      Caching dependency downloads and Docker layers, and running
      independent stages in parallel, are usually enough to keep even a
      large pipeline under ten minutes without cutting any real safety
      checks.
    </p>

    <div class="related-tools">
      <h2>Related tools on this site</h2>
      <p>Put this guide into practice with these free tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/devops/docker-compose-validator">Docker Compose Validator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/devops/dockerfile-generator">Dockerfile Generator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/devops/yaml-validator">YAML Validator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/interview-qa/#questions">DevOps Scenario Interview Q&amp;A</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .container { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 820px); margin-inline: auto; }
  .hero { position: relative; overflow: hidden; padding: 56px 0 46px; background: radial-gradient(circle at 85% 15%, rgba(56, 189, 248, .2), transparent 26%), linear-gradient(135deg, #0b1220, #111d33 62%, #162a4f); color: #fff; }
  .hero:after { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 38px 38px; }
  .hero-content { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  .back { display: block; margin-bottom: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none; font-size: .92rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #fff; }
  .eyebrow { display: inline-block; color: #66d3ff; font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .hero h1 { margin: 12px 0 16px; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.03em; }
  .hero p { margin: 0; max-width: 700px; color: #c8d5e7; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.8; }
  .meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin-top: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-size: .87rem; font-weight: 700; }

  .article-wrap { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 760px); margin: 0 auto; padding: 48px 0 90px; }
  .toc { margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 22px 24px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 14px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .toc h2 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: .82rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #2563eb; }
  .toc ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; }
  .toc li { margin-bottom: 7px; }
  .toc a { color: #334155; font-weight: 650; text-decoration: none; }
  .toc a:hover { color: #2563eb; }

  .article-wrap h2 { margin: 44px 0 14px; color: #111827; font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.3; scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .article-wrap h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .article-wrap p { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #374151; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.85; }
  .article-wrap ol, .article-wrap ul { margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 22px; color: #374151; line-height: 1.8; }
  .article-wrap li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .article-wrap code { padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 5px; background: #eef2f7; color: #0f172a; font: 600 .88em ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }
  .article-wrap pre { margin: 0 0 20px; padding: 17px 19px; border-radius: 12px; overflow-x: auto; background: #0d1524; color: #dce8f7; }
  .article-wrap pre code { padding: 0; background: none; color: inherit; font: 500 .87rem/1.7 ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }

  .related-tools { margin-top: 54px; padding: 26px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 16px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .related-tools h2 { margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .related-tools p { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools ul { list-style: none; margin: 16px 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
  .related-tools li { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools a { display: block; padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid #cbd7e7; border-radius: 10px; background: #fff; color: #1d4ed8; font-weight: 800; font-size: .9rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .related-tools a:hover { border-color: #2563eb; background: #eff6ff; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero { padding: 44px 0 36px; }
  }
</style>
